---
title: Journal
description: Book entries by hand, with VAT, and find everything nullish booked for you.
---

The journal lists every entry in your books. Most of them nullish books for you: invoices, payments, reconciled bank movements, year-end closing. For the rest, you add entries yourself.

## Book an entry

{% steps %}

1. Go to **Accounting → Journal** and create a new entry.

2. Set the **Date** and, if you like, a **Reference**, e.g. a receipt number.

3. Add a line per booking: the **Debit** and **Credit** accounts, a **Description** and the **Amount**.

4. Pick a **VAT rate** if VAT applies. nullish adds the VAT lines for you.

5. Check that **Difference** is zero, then save.

{% /steps %}

A line can also have only a debit or only a credit account, for entries split across several lines. For a foreign currency, pick the **Currency** and enter the **Exchange rate**.

{% aside %}
An entry must balance, have a reference or a description, and fall in an open
[period](/en/accounting/periods/).
{% /aside %}

## Duplicate

Booking the same thing every month, like rent? Open last month's entry and choose **Duplicate**. You get a new entry prefilled with the same lines: adjust the date and save.

## What can't be changed

Entries are numbered automatically, one after the other. Some are locked to keep your books consistent:

- entries booked by an invoice: void or write off the [invoice](/en/billing/invoices/) instead;
- entries of a reconciled bank movement;
- year-end closing entries, and everything in a closed period;
- entries imported by a migration.

Everything else you can edit or delete.

## Find an entry

Filter the journal by **Number**, date range or **Reference**. Each entry shows where it comes from, e.g. **Invoice opening**, **Invoice payment** or **Opening balance**, and links to the invoice or bank movement behind it.
